**Reported upstream by conflab-vc on hv's explicit instruction; full evidence in Conflab issue `0011` (HIGH). One of four filed separately: `0124` (prose discarded), `0125` (this), `0126` (note field absent), `0127` (delimiter injected).**

**THIS IS THE CLASS THAT CHANGES THE SCOPE OF ALL FOUR, and the reason is arithmetic: a row can LOSE text and GAIN text in one field, so a length delta NETS them and reports nothing.** Any instrument that measures this damage by comparing lengths is blind to it by construction -- including the one that found `0124`.

**MEASURED ON CONFLAB.** ST0121/AT-02.4 carries THREE kinds of damage in one 1363-character field:
- **head lost** -- the field opens mid-sentence, with 3 close parens against 2 open;
- **middle DUPLICATED** -- a 92-character span appears TWICE in canon and ONCE in `acceptance.v2.md`. conflab-vc controlled that by counting a second phrase: 1 against 2;
- **tail truncated** mid-phrase.

**Second instance in a DIFFERENT thread:** ST0123/AT-01.5, a 104-character span duplicated. **So it is neither a one-off nor confined to one thread.**

**A CANDIDATE MECHANISM FOR THE DUPLICATION, DERIVED BY vc FROM READING THIS REPOSITORY'S SOURCE AND NOT YET DRIVEN -- stated as a hypothesis, because a plausible cause that nobody drove is exactly what this estate keeps learning not to trust:**

`note()` captures everything after the first ` -- ` that follows ` -- status: `. If a ` -- covers ... ` clause sits AFTER `status:` on the row, that captured tail INCLUDES the covers clause and any prose written inside it. Separately, `Covers.qualifiers` collects that same in-clause prose, and `with_qualifiers()` then folds it as `{id}: {q}` and **APPENDS it to the note**:

```rust
fn with_qualifiers(note: Option<String>, qualifiers: &[(String, String)]) -> Option<String> {
  if qualifiers.is_empty() { return note; }
  let folded = qualifiers.iter().map(|(id, q)| format!("{id}: {q}"))
    .collect::<Vec<_>>().join(" -- ");
  append_note(note, &folded)
}
```

**So the same authored span can reach canon twice by two routes -- once inside the captured tail, once appended by the qualifier fold.** That would produce exactly the observed shape: a span present twice in canon and once in the source. **WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT: a duplicated span in a row whose `covers` clause precedes `status:`, or one carrying no qualifier prose at all.** Drive it before fixing it.

**THE HEAD LOSS IS NOT EXPLAINED by that hypothesis** and may be a separate cause. It is filed here rather than as a fifth issue only because it was measured in the same field of the same row; **if the duplication resolves and the head loss does not, split it.**

**WHY THIS ONE IS THE WORST OF THE FOUR:** `0124` removes authored text, which is recoverable from a preserved source. This one puts text into a ratified contract **in a position its author never wrote it**, and the result still reads as prose. A reader has no signal at all, and the usual instrument -- compare the lengths -- returns clean.

**NOT CLAIMED:** this class has NOT been swept. Both instances were found incidentally while reading rows for something else. The population is unknown and is not bounded by the 20 rows `0124` counts.
